  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mvalidateForm[39m(formEl, rules) [32m// valuesFromForm + validate -> { valid, errors, values }[39m

  [38;5;153mrules[39m is an object mapping field name to an array of rule functions. [38;5;153merrors[39m is an object keyed by field name holding
  the first message for that field; [38;5;153mvalid[39m is [38;5;153merrors[39m being empty. A field with no entry in [38;5;153mrules[39m is not validated; a rule
  for a field absent from the form sees [38;5;153mundefined[39m and so fails [38;5;153mrequired[39m, which is the behavior you want for a typo'd
  field name.

  Two deliberate omissions, both easy to add when a form needs them: no [38;5;153mpattern[39m/[38;5;153memail[39m/[38;5;153mmatches[39m built-ins (login needs
  neither), and no async rules. Custom checks don't wait for me to add them — a rule is just a function, so a form can
  pass its own inline.

  [38;5;153mvaluesFromForm[39m reads [38;5;153mformEl.elements[39m by [38;5;153mname[39m, skipping unnamed and submit-type elements, and trims strings. That's the
  one place DOM knowledge lives.
